WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.170
The war was over, the treaty was signed, and the empire they were fighting for had already sold the islands out from under them.

2
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:16.100
Fifty men inside a stone church went on holding a country that no longer existed for eleven more months, calling every newspaper that told them the truth a forgery.

3
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.070
What does it take to make a soldier believe his war has ended?

4
00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:35.280
The 57 men behind the walls of the church feared the newspapers the enemy threw at them more than they feared the enemy's bullets.

5
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:44.210
Because those newspapers said only one thing: the country you are dying for lost this war months ago, and this ground no longer belongs to Spain.

6
00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:51.220
The lieutenant decided the pages were a trap, tore them up, and went on fighting — for 337 days.

7
00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:59.510
On the eastern shore of the island of Luzon, at the edge of a bay that opens onto the wide waters of the Pacific, there is a small town called Baler.

8
00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:04.280
Today you can drive there, and surfers know its beaches.

9
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.850
But at the end of the 19th century, Baler was one of the loneliest corners of the Philippines.

10
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:19.100
The Sierra Madre mountains cut the town off from the rest of the island: forested, carved by rain, a barrier that took weeks to cross even on the back of a mule.

11
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:25.790
The only reasonable way to travel from Manila to Baler was by sea, and even that was not possible in every season.

12
00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:33.120
Between November and February the swells rolling in from the Pacific beat the bay so hard that no steamer could come alongside.

13
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:40.770
For part of every year, in other words, Baler was a dot that existed on the empire's map but could not actually be reached.

14
00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:44.740
Spain had governed the Philippines for more than 300 years.

15
00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:53.510
In the towns at the far edge of that government, the state meant a handful of officials, a friary church, and an understrength detachment of soldiers.

16
00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:56.360
Baler was no different.

17
00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:03.690
In the middle of the town stood a church of stone, thick-walled, with few windows: the church of San Luis de Tolosa.

18
00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:11.500
In a tropical climate a stone building is a luxury; the Baler church sat among the surrounding houses of wood and thatch like a boulder.

19
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.110
No one had designed it as a fortress, but it stood like one.

20
00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.640
The town was not calm.

21
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:26.610
The Philippine revolution that began in 1896 had spread across the whole island, and Baler had taken its share.

22
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:34.260
In the autumn of 1897 revolutionaries stormed the Spanish detachment in the town and the garrison suffered heavy losses.

23
00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.750
Madrid wanted the lost position back.

24
00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:45.720
So in February of 1898, on the 12th, a steamer dropped anchor in the bay and a new detachment came ashore.

25
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.970
These were the men the whole world would eventually talk about, though nobody knew it that day.

26
00:02:50.970 --> 00:03:00.860
At their head was Captain Enrique de las Morenas y Fossi, appointed civil and military governor of the district, which made him both commander and administrator.

27
00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:06.910
He had two lieutenants with him: Juan Alonso Zayas and Saturnino Martín Cerezo.

28
00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:11.520
A military physician came along, Rogelio Vigil de Quiñones.

29
00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.450
And a chaplain, Cándido Gómez Carreño.

30
00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:20.100
Counting the enlisted men, they were 57 in all.

31
00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:28.390
Most were young, most were peasants, most had been conscripted out of the poor Spanish countryside and had never seen an ocean in their lives.

32
00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:31.720
The first months passed in a strange quiet.

33
00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:37.610
The soldiers traded with the townspeople, children ran across the square, the church bell rang.

34
00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:43.020
Captain Las Morenas saw to the town's affairs, kept his ledgers, wrote his reports.

35
00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:52.910
Martín Cerezo looked at the church's walls, its door, its bell tower, and kept to himself the thought that went through his mind: if it came to it, this place could be held.

36
00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:55.920
It was the reflex of a soldier.

37
00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.170
That reflex would be the reason 57 men stayed alive a few months later.

38
00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:06.740
Meanwhile, thousands of kilometers away, the wheel of history had begun to turn.

39
00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:14.070
In February an American battleship blew up in Havana harbor; in April the United States and Spain went to war.

40
00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:22.520
On the morning of May 1, 1898, the Spanish squadron faced the American navy in Manila Bay and was destroyed within a few hours.

41
00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:29.050
Spain's 300-year presence in the Pacific was shaken to its foundations in the course of a single morning.

42
00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:33.340
Baler heard about it weeks later, in broken fragments of rumor.

43
00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:40.190
The news reaching the town now came from one direction only: over the mountains, out of the mouths of the insurgents.

44
00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:47.200
No official notice, no order, no mail arrived from Manila, because Manila itself was under siege.

45
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.970
The detachment was left at the end of the empire with no one to tell it what had happened to it.

46
00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:55.300
When June came the mood of the town changed.

47
00:04:55.300 --> 00:05:00.710
Trade fell off, greetings grew short, the streets began emptying early.

48
00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:05.640
At night the soldiers listened to the sounds coming out of the forest.

49
00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:15.050
Captain Las Morenas ordered the supplies carried into the church: rice, dried meat, beans, salt, kerosene, ammunition.

50
00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:23.500
The townspeople packed up their houses and withdrew in ones and twos; a town abandoned by its own people is the quietest possible warning of what is coming.

51
00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:27.310
In the last days of June, Baler was all but empty.

52
00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:30.480
The only solid thing left was the stone church.

53
00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:37.810
Fifty-seven men, with no idea that an empire on the other side of the world had already been beaten, walked inside it.

54
00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:44.660
On the morning of June 30, 1898, Martín Cerezo went out toward the river with a small patrol.

55
00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:47.510
He wanted to see why the town had emptied.

56
00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:57.240
The reason showed itself soon enough: fire opened from among the trees, the patrol was ambushed, a corporal was wounded, and the men pulled back fighting.

57
00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:03.290
That withdrawal was no longer the beginning of a garrison's duty; it was the beginning of a siege.

58
00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:07.100
The detachment closed the church door behind them and barred it.

59
00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:12.350
The next day, July 1, came the first call to surrender.

60
00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:22.560
The Filipino commanders sent a written letter: Spanish rule in the Philippines was finished, resistance was pointless, and those who gave themselves up would be treated honorably.

61
00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:31.650
One of the names at the head of the siege was a son of the town itself, Teodorico Novicio Luna; a few weeks later Calixto Villacorta took over the command.

62
00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:37.700
The force under their orders varied between 800 and 1000 men at different stages of the siege.

63
00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:42.470
Inside there were 57, and their number would fall with every passing day.

64
00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:45.960
Captain Las Morenas refused the call.

65
00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:54.250
The reasoning behind the refusal was a piece of military logic that looks like stubbornness from the outside and is something entirely different from the inside.

66
00:06:54.250 --> 00:07:02.060
A Spanish officer could surrender a besieged position only on a written and verifiable order coming down from his own superiors.

67
00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:11.310
The sentence "the war is over," spoken by the enemy, was by definition a ruse of war; every army's manual on sieges carries examples of it.

68
00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:22.000
The tragedy of Baler was born precisely there: these men did not act wrongly, they acted correctly — only, acting correctly kept them fighting for nothing for 11 months.

69
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.330
The church quickly became a fortified work.

70
00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:33.780
Loopholes were cut into the walls, the door and windows were blocked with sandbags and sacks of rubble, the bell tower became an observation post.

71
00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:39.830
The houses around the church gave cover to the attackers, so the detachment set them on fire in night sorties.

72
00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:44.600
To stay alive, they burned their own town with their own hands.

73
00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:53.050
When the fires had burned out there was an empty, level, open space around the church: a space a sentry could see across and a rifle could reach.

74
00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:57.340
Then the siege settled into its own monotonous rhythm.

75
00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:03.710
Firing before dawn, a sniper's duel through the day, silence toward evening.

76
00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:09.920
The besiegers could not batter the wall with artillery; the light guns they had could only open wounds in the stone.

77
00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.690
When they tried a direct assault they took heavy losses crossing the open ground.

78
00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:25.220
Over the 337 days of the siege, Filipino losses in dead and wounded are reckoned at around 700, which is the clearest measure of how difficult a target the church was.

79
00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:29.670
The relationship between the two sides was not made of bullets alone.

80
00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:38.600
From time to time the firing stopped, men met under a white flag, tobacco and small gifts changed hands, they asked after each other's health.

81
00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:42.410
Then everyone went back to his position and started shooting again.

82
00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:50.700
This strange courtesy, common enough in sieges, lasted at Baler from beginning to end, and it also decided how the end would come.

83
00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:54.030
In August the world outside picked up speed.

84
00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:58.160
On August 13 Manila surrendered to American forces.

85
00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.450
Spain no longer even had a capital to defend in the Philippines.

86
00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:08.820
The Spanish authorities in Manila did not forget Baler; on the contrary, they sent emissaries again and again.

87
00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:16.150
Ranking officers, signed letters, even two Spanish friars held prisoner by the Filipinos were sent to the church.

88
00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:22.840
The friars were let inside and stayed there until the end of the siege, but they could not make the news they carried stick.

89
00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:28.890
Because for the men inside, every emissary was an emissary who had passed through the enemy's hands.

90
00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:33.500
Every letter was a piece of paper that had come from behind the enemy's lines.

91
00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:40.990
Signatures could be imitated, seals could be manufactured, a captured officer could be made to write whatever sentence you wanted.

92
00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:46.240
Martín Cerezo considered each of these possibilities and rejected them all.

93
00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:55.810
In the memoir he would write years later he describes the one fixed idea in his mind during those days: proof had to be proof that had not passed through the enemy's hands.

94
00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:58.340
There was no such proof.

95
00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:03.910
And inside the church, an enemy far more patient than any bullet had already gone to work.

96
00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:06.920
The real killer of the siege was not the rifle.

97
00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:13.770
The provisions had been weighed out from the start: rice, dried beans, salted meat, a little tinned food.

98
00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:18.860
As the months passed the rations were cut, then halved, then became unrecognizable.

99
00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:22.670
But the real problem was not quantity, it was variety.

100
00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:27.280
For months the men in the church ate almost nothing but polished rice.

101
00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:37.810
Today we know what that means: when the bran is stripped from rice, vitamin B1 goes with it, and without that vitamin the human body surrenders to the disease called beriberi.

102
00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:40.180
Beriberi comes slowly.

103
00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:43.830
First numbness in the legs, then weakness.

104
00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:47.480
A man can no longer lift his rifle.

105
00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:54.010
Starting at the ankles the body swells, the edema climbs upward, the breath tightens.

106
00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:56.860
In the end the heart tires and stops.

107
00:10:56.860 --> 00:11:02.430
Inside the church, healthy 20-year-old men became unable to walk within weeks.

108
00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:10.560
Laid out along the base of the walls, their swollen legs stretched out in front of them, without the strength left to speak, they died one after another.

109
00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:21.250
The physician Rogelio Vigil de Quiñones did not know the cause of the disease; nobody knew it exactly at that date, because the concept of a vitamin had not yet been established.

110
00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:23.940
He had no medicine left either.

111
00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:30.790
But with a good doctor's instinct he noticed something: there was a link between the illness and what they were eating.

112
00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:33.320
He insisted on fresh food.

113
00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:43.690
So a small vegetable patch was planted around the church, and raids were mounted in the dark to slip into the nearby gardens and gather squash, greens, whatever could be found.

114
00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:48.460
Going out under fire for a handful of leaves looks insane from the outside.

115
00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:53.710
From the inside it was survival itself, and those raids really did slow the dying.

116
00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:56.080
It was not enough.

117
00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:00.850
The chaplain, Cándido Gómez Carreño, was taken by the disease.

118
00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:07.860
On October 18, 1898, Lieutenant Juan Alonso Zayas died; beriberi had taken him too.

119
00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:16.150
Within a month, on November 22, the commander of the detachment, Captain Enrique de las Morenas, died of the same illness.

120
00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:22.360
He had come as the governor of a town; he died inside a church, beside his own men, without medicine.

121
00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:25.850
That November is the darkest month of Baler.

122
00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:31.740
A detachment that loses two of its officers at once would, under ordinary circumstances, fall apart.

123
00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:36.030
The bodies were buried in the churchyard, in pits dug under fire.

124
00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:42.080
Even burying the dead was an operation; short truces were arranged and the earth was thrown over in haste.

125
00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:51.170
The number of those who died during the siege reached 19: 15 from disease, 2 from wounds, and 2 by the decision of their own comrades.

126
00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:56.900
Command had now passed to the only officer left, Saturnino Martín Cerezo.

127
00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:01.990
He was in his early thirties, a provincial man, hard, and sparing with words.

128
00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:12.040
What came into his hands was not a unit but a wreck: sick men who could not walk, dwindling supplies, two dead officers, and an enemy outside shouting endlessly that the war was over.

129
00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:16.970
What Martín Cerezo did with it was a choice that raises the hair on the back of your neck.

130
00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:20.300
Instead of loosening his grip, he tightened it.

131
00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:30.830
He reorganized the watches, kept up the daily roll call, gave a duty to every man still able to stand, and tried to run discipline exactly as it had run on the first day of the siege.

132
00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:33.200
There was a reason for this.

133
00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:41.010
In places like that, what hastens death is usually not disease but dissolution; a man with nothing left to do goes faster.

134
00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:45.140
Routine functioned as a kind of medicine inside that church.

135
00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:50.710
Morning roll call, night watch, watering the vegetable patch, cleaning out a loophole.

136
00:13:50.710 --> 00:14:03.000
But that same discipline, that same hardness, would leave a permanent argument hanging around Martín Cerezo's name: a hero, or a stubborn man who kept his soldiers lying down in front of death for months without need?

137
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:06.490
That question is not entirely closed even today.

138
00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:11.420
What is certain is that the men inside the church did not know what they were holding out for.

139
00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:18.110
While they were dying at the foot of those walls, Spain had sat down at a table and was selling its colony.

140
00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:23.200
On December 10, 1898, a treaty was signed in Paris.

141
00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:32.610
Spain gave up its sovereignty over Cuba, handed over Puerto Rico and Guam, and left the Philippines to the United States in return for 20 million dollars.

142
00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:38.340
The sovereignty being defended by the church at Baler came to a legal end that day on a sheet of paper.

143
00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:43.270
The men inside knew nothing about it, and if they had, they would not have believed it.

144
00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:46.760
Because disbelief had become a habit in that church.

145
00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:52.330
For some time the besiegers had been trying a new method: paper instead of bullets.

146
00:14:52.330 --> 00:14:57.900
Coming forward under a white flag, they left Spanish-language newspapers in front of the church.

147
00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:04.110
Newspapers reporting that Manila had fallen, that the fleet had been sunk, that a treaty had been signed.

148
00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:08.080
Martín Cerezo read them and judged them to be forgeries.

149
00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:11.410
Seen from today that decision looks unbelievable.

150
00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:14.260
But it is worth following his logic.

151
00:15:14.260 --> 00:15:18.230
A newspaper is the easiest thing in the world to counterfeit.

152
00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:23.960
If there is a printing press behind the enemy's lines, you can set and print whatever news you like.

153
00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:33.210
What is more, some of the papers that arrived really did carry news that was suspiciously well-aimed: pages that seemed chosen precisely to convince the man inside.

154
00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:44.700
Martín Cerezo went through each issue like an intelligence officer, studying the advertisements, the dates, the typesetting, and in every one of them he found the possibility of a fabrication.

155
00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:50.750
He threw the newspapers aside, and kept most of them from his men in case they damaged morale.

156
00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:54.080
Meanwhile the world outside changed once more.

157
00:15:54.080 --> 00:16:02.690
On February 4, 1899, fighting broke out in Manila between Filipino and American forces, and the Philippine-American War began.

158
00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:10.500
The people besieging the church were now inside a brand-new war, and they wanted this strange knot at Baler untied as quickly as possible.

159
00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:14.150
In April an unexpected shadow appeared from the sea.

160
00:16:14.150 --> 00:16:20.360
On April 11, 1899, the American warship Yorktown anchored in Baler bay.

161
00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:26.730
The Americans had heard the story of the Spaniards still holding out in the church and were thinking of taking them off.

162
00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:33.580
A boat was lowered from the ship; a small party under Lieutenant James Gillmore moved toward the mouth of the river.

163
00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:37.710
Filipino troops waiting behind the trees on the shore opened fire.

164
00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:46.320
Several of the Americans were killed, Gillmore and his men were taken prisoner; they spent months in captivity and were rescued only in December.

165
00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:51.090
The American ship waited in the bay for a while, then weighed anchor and left.

166
00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:54.260
The men in the church heard those shots.

167
00:16:54.260 --> 00:17:01.110
Rescue from the sea had been torn apart a few kilometers from their door, and they never even fully understood what it was.

168
00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:05.720
By the time May came, the air inside the church had turned poisonous.

169
00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:15.450
Among men who had spent months between the same walls, seeing the same faces, burying their dead, living half-starved, despair had begun to be spoken aloud.

170
00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:21.820
On May 8 a soldier, a private named Alcaide, slipped out in the dark and went over to the Filipino side.

171
00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:28.670
The escape was the most dangerous moment of the siege, because if one man had run, it meant others were thinking about it.

172
00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:34.400
Martín Cerezo took this as a sign of dissolution and convened a court-martial.

173
00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:43.650
Two soldiers found to have helped the deserter, Antonio Menache Sánchez and Vicente González Toca, were shot on June 1, 1899.

174
00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:48.900
One day before the last day of the siege, they died by their own comrades' bullets.

175
00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:56.230
This is the most disputed, darkest page of the Baler story, and the fact hardest to fit inside a narrative of heroism.

176
00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:59.560
And yet the lock had already begun to turn.

177
00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:12.170
On May 28, 1899, Lieutenant Colonel Cristóbal Aguilar y Castañeda, sent from Manila, approached the church under a white flag and tried to talk as everyone else had, and went back without result.

178
00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:15.340
But he left a bundle of newspapers behind him.

179
00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:19.470
Martín Cerezo opened these papers with suspicion too.

180
00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:22.160
He turned the pages.

181
00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:30.770
Then, not among the war reports but in the pages of daily life, he came across a small, ordinary, trivial item about an officer he knew.

182
00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:35.060
It was a private detail of the kind only he could have known.

183
00:18:35.060 --> 00:18:44.790
No enemy could have invented it; inventing it would have required knowing that man's life, and besides, it would never have occurred to anyone to put a detail like that inside a trap.

184
00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:48.120
Holding the paper in his hand, he understood.

185
00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:50.490
The newspaper was real.

186
00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:54.460
And if this newspaper was real, the others were real too.

187
00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:56.830
Spain was gone.

188
00:18:56.830 --> 00:19:01.760
For nearly a year they had been guarding the flag of an empire that no longer existed.

189
00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:09.090
On the morning of June 2, 1899, a flag was waved from the walls of the Baler church.

190
00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:14.820
For the first time in months, the men inside were waiting without raising their rifles.

191
00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:19.110
Martín Cerezo did not surrender; he negotiated.

192
00:19:19.110 --> 00:19:30.600
He set out his terms in writing: firing would stop, the weapons would be handed over to Filipino officers in good order, he and his men would not be counted as prisoners of war, no one would be harmed,

193
00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:36.170
and safe passage would be provided to a port from which they could return to Spain.

194
00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:42.700
The Filipino officers running the command in the last stage of the siege accepted the terms.

195
00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:45.070
Then the bugle sounded.

196
00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:50.320
The flag that had stood on its pole since the first day of the siege was brought down and folded.

197
00:19:50.320 --> 00:20:02.610
The rubble sacks and sandbags in front of the door were cleared away; the door that had been shut for 337 days was opened, and those who came out into the daylight were counted: 33 men.

198
00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:06.100
Fifty-seven had gone into the church.

199
00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:10.230
Nineteen had died, 5 had deserted.

200
00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:16.600
Those left were bearded, sallow-faced, some too weak to walk, their uniforms in rags.

201
00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:21.370
What waited for them outside was not the humiliating scene of a defeat ceremony.

202
00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:29.340
The Filipino soldiers who had been firing at those walls for months formed up, presented arms, and saluted the wretched column in front of them.

203
00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:31.710
The townspeople brought food.

204
00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:38.240
The 33 set out from Baler under the protection of the men who had besieged them, and reached Manila safe and sound.

205
00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:42.530
The document that turned the story into a legend came four weeks later.

206
00:20:42.530 --> 00:20:50.180
On June 30, 1899, Emilio Aguinaldo, president of the Philippine Republic, issued a decree.

207
00:20:50.180 --> 00:21:00.070
The decree praised the resistance shown by the Spanish force defending the Baler church, describing it as an epic worthy of the descendants of the legendary heroes El Cid and Pelayo.

208
00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:10.760
And it laid down this ruling: the individuals making up that force were not to be considered prisoners but rather friends, and were to be given the papers necessary to return to their own country.

209
00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:19.690
It is worth remembering that this was written by a man whose people had fought against 300 years of colonial rule, and that he wrote it for the soldiers of his own enemy.

210
00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:28.620
Aguinaldo was at that moment in a life-or-death war with the American army; releasing the 33 men at Baler brought him no military advantage whatsoever.

211
00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:35.310
The decree was not a calculation but a gesture, and history tends to forget calculations and remember gestures.

212
00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:40.240
The survivors reached Barcelona on September 1, 1899.

213
00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:42.290
There was a crowd on the quay.

214
00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:53.780
It was a strange moment for Spain: a country beaten on every front in 1898, having lost the last pieces of its empire, found a single story to hold on to and held on to it.

215
00:21:53.780 --> 00:22:02.390
Martín Cerezo was promoted to major and given one of the heaviest of the military decorations; the enlisted men received medals and pensions.

216
00:22:02.390 --> 00:22:11.320
In 1904 Martín Cerezo published his memoir of the siege, and many of the details we know about Baler today first came out of those pages.

217
00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:18.330
The name given to them in Spanish stuck as well: los últimos de Filipinas, the Last Ones of the Philippines.

218
00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:22.940
And it is exactly here that the story began its second life.

219
00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:31.870
A film shot in 1945 carried the siege onto the screen as a national epic and turned the men in the church into a whole generation's definition of heroism.

220
00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:42.240
A new film made in 2016 told the same events from the opposite direction: as the story of young men spent inside a war that was already over, of a loyalty that had lost its meaning,

221
00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:46.690
of ordinary people paying the bill for colonial adventures.

222
00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:56.100
The two films describe the same 337 days; the difference between them is the change that took place in 70 years in the way Spain looks at its own past.

223
00:22:56.100 --> 00:23:02.630
The last of those who came out of the siege alive, Emilio Fabregat, died in 1960.

224
00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:12.520
By the time he died, the Philippines was an independent republic, Spain's colonial age had been closed into the history books, and the church at Baler was still standing.

225
00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:16.490
Today Baler is the capital of Aurora province.

226
00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:24.140
The church has been restored, a monument to the siege stands in the square, and the place where the Spanish soldiers were buried is marked.

227
00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:35.790
In 2003 the Congress of the Philippines passed a law declaring June 30 Philippine-Spanish Friendship Day; the date chosen is precisely the date of Aguinaldo's "not prisoners but friends" decree.

228
00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:45.360
Every year on that day the flags of the two countries are raised side by side in Baler, and the descendants of two armies that once fired on each other stand in front of the same church.

229
00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:50.290
What makes Baler worth remembering is not only the endurance of 33 men.

230
00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:53.300
The real issue is the news itself.

231
00:23:53.300 --> 00:24:01.430
Inside that church a war went on for 11 months after it had ended, because word of the ending could not reach the place in a form that could be accepted.

232
00:24:01.430 --> 00:24:09.240
Today it takes seconds for an event to be heard, but a human being accepting a piece of news is still an entirely separate matter.

233
00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:20.570
The newspapers in Martín Cerezo's hands were true, and for months he did not believe them; what convinced him in the end was not some overwhelming proof but a detail too small for anyone to have invented.

234
00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:25.820
The human mind is most often taken by the greatest truth through the smallest detail.

235
00:24:25.820 --> 00:24:29.790
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

236
00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:33.920
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
